✅ UI Testing Bug in Production :
When a production bug is reported, my first step is to review the test scenarios written for that feature to verify whether the scenario was actually covered in our functional or regression suites.
If the test is already covered, I check the CI/CD history — specifically, the last green build and identify when it started failing. I correlate that with any recent PR merges or deployments to see if the bug was introduced due to a code change.
If it's not due to recent merges, I check for environment-specific issues by running the same test case in a staging or lower environment to rule out config issues or test flakiness.
I then proceed with a detailed root cause analysis (RCA) — inspecting logs, server responses, frontend console errors (if UI-related), and any data-related dependencies that might be breaking the flow.
Meanwhile, I make sure to collaborate with the dev team to give them exact reproduction steps, test data, and screenshots/logs — so the issue can be fixed quickly.
After the fix is made, I ensure complete regression is done for related areas, and we update or add new test coverage if needed to prevent the issue from happening again.”


A production bug was reported where users were unable to save changes in the Class Set feature,
even though the UI showed a success message. My first step was to check our Cypress regression suite and confirm that
we had a test for editing and saving Class Sets. The test had passed in the last green build,
so I reviewed the CI/CD history and noticed that the failure started after a recent deployment that included a backend API update.
To rule out environment issues, I ran the same Cypress test in our staging environment and found it failed there as well.
I then checked the browser console and network logs, which revealed a 400 error from the save API due to a missing required field in the request payload—something that had changed in the backend contract.
I immediately shared the reproduction steps, test data, and error logs with the development team.
They quickly fixed the API contract and updated the frontend to match. After the fix,
I ran a full regression on the Class Set module and added a new test to specifically check for required fields in the payload, ensuring this issue wouldn’t recur.





